WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.824 --> 00:00:05.836
I don't think I'm the only one with
a little bit of PTSD these days.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:10.110
For a lot of us that lived through
those really dark days, suddenly,

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.606
here we are again with a virus you
can't see randomly affecting people,

00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:18.014
life being put on hold,
all sorts of things changing.

00:00:18.014 --> 00:00:22.318
Around the time of the lockdown here in
San Francisco, it suddenly occurred to me

00:00:22.318 --> 00:00:25.248
that this is going to be
the second pandemic of my life.

00:00:25.248 --> 00:00:28.515
And really, no one should have
to live through more than one.

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:31.865
[MUSIC]

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:35.877
All those years ago in San Francisco,
in the early 80s, and mid 80s,

00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:37.321
it was a really dark time.

00:00:37.321 --> 00:00:41.445
I had just finished Stanford Business
School and had taken care of a friend and

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:43.417
a classmate who died during school.

00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:48.345
And moved up to San Francisco without
really thinking much about a career and

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:51.810
a job, a little heartbroken,
a little bit lost, and

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:55.670
kind of stumbled into
the middle an epidemic.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.880
The Castro back then was horrible.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.632
You'd walk down the street and you'd pass
dozens of people who you knew you were

00:01:01.632 --> 00:01:03.640
probably never going to see again.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.775
The country didn't seem to be
responding or didn't seem to be caring.

00:01:05.775 --> 00:01:09.408
When some friends and
I started the AIDS Memorial Quilt,

00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:11.370
it was really a cry for help.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.300
It was really a way to
be heard at a time when

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.990
we all thought we were going
to die alone and forgotten.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.704
A friend of a friend, Cleve Jones,
came to me in 1987 and

00:01:21.704 --> 00:01:25.864
said he had this crazy idea for a quilt
and some way to reach Middle America with

00:01:25.864 --> 00:01:29.520
something that's not threatening and
feels warm and comforting.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.741
And you think of your grandmother
when someone says quilt, and

00:01:32.741 --> 00:01:35.919
that wasn't the world we were
living in with AIDS right then.

00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:39.824
It was a much different world where people
were either there's you and there's us,

00:01:39.824 --> 00:01:43.570
and we don't have it and you do,
and that's all there is to it.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:47.530
Almost immediately, I thought this is
an idea that's really going to resonate.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:51.087
We said we were going to do that first
display at the March on Washington for

00:01:51.087 --> 00:01:53.220
Gay and Lesbian Rights in October of 87,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.094
four months after we decided to start
building this thing, which was crazy.

00:01:57.094 --> 00:02:01.820
And I think I thought we would do this for
a little while, and we'd make our point,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.120
and that would be the end.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.450
And I don't think we were prepared for
the beauty of the quilts.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.394
We had been making something that were
basically bed sheets to make a protest

00:02:11.394 --> 00:02:11.902
statement.

00:02:11.902 --> 00:02:16.482
And suddenly we had mothers all over
the country, sending us panels for

00:02:16.482 --> 00:02:22.530
their dead son, expecting us to preserve
them and care for them in perpetuity.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:28.465
I never thought it would become my life's
work, but it has become my life's work.

00:02:28.465 --> 00:02:31.801
In the middle of all of this COVID,
when it first happened,

00:02:31.801 --> 00:02:34.537
I wasn't the only one that
was kind of looking for

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:39.540
a way to be helpful, a way to do something
which has always been kind of my mode.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.590
Gert McMullin, who'd been very
involved in sewing together

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.490
many of the panels in the quilt for
years, was feeling the same way.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.940
She used to be able to go into
hospital rooms in the 80s and

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.455
be with dying friends.

00:02:50.455 --> 00:02:53.950
She just couldn't stand that
she couldn't touch people and

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.690
she couldn't be up close with people.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.090
Finally she said, I'm going to go
into the workshop, it's empty,

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.090
there's nobody else there.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.310
And I'm going to start making some face
masks and we can give them away to people.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.810
I made a phone call to an old friend, and

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:09.600
it turned out she was working at
an agency that was desperate for masks.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.397
This project has really opened my
eyes to see how many vulnerable and

00:03:13.397 --> 00:03:17.857
at risk populations there are in an
epidemic like this, in a homeless shelter,

00:03:17.857 --> 00:03:19.339
in a youth rehab program.

00:03:19.339 --> 00:03:21.765
People are right there up
close with each other and

00:03:21.765 --> 00:03:23.570
you can't get that kind of distance.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.870
The AIDS Memorial Quilt has
gone from a protest banner and

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.460
something we were going to take to
Washington to make a difference for

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.750
one day to a national treasure.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.430
I mean, it's the largest community
arts project in the world.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.230
More people have worked on
the quilt than built the pyramids.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.593
And a lot of what we did at
the time was not just the fabric.

00:03:44.593 --> 00:03:47.566
We inspired people to do something,
anything,

00:03:47.566 --> 00:03:52.180
to express what they were going through,
to tell their stories.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.470
And I think now about where we are with
this mask project and it's the same thing.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.190
We may make 2 or 3,000 masks and
that may save some lives.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.843
I'd much rather have
this be a catalyst for

00:04:01.843 --> 00:04:05.092
other people to feel like I
can find something to do.

00:04:05.092 --> 00:04:09.090
I can get out of my house and
go help stuff food at the food bank.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.270
If we can do something
simple like make masks,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:15.060
you can do something simple,
like find a way to be helpful.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.522
And that's what I'd much rather be
remembered for, being that catalyst,

00:04:18.522 --> 00:04:20.048
than for just doing some sewing.

00:04:20.048 --> 00:04:30.048
[MUSIC]

